    "description": "h2. Summary\n\nEnterprise platform teams need a UI-based experience for managing MCP server entries in the MCP Catalog, replacing the current YAML-only workflow. Today, adding, editing, or removing MCP servers from the catalog requires direct YAML file manipulation, which blocks non-technical personas and is inconsistent with the rest of the MCP management experience, which is moving toward UI-driven workflows.\n\nh2. Problem Statement\n\nThe MCP Catalog currently requires administrators to manually edit a YAML configuration to add or modify MCP server entries. This creates several problems:\n\n* *Non-technical personas are blocked.* Platform operators, security reviewers, and team leads who need to curate the catalog for their organization cannot do so without YAML editing skills or developer assistance.\n* *Inconsistent management experience.* Other aspects of end-to-end MCP lifecycle management (registration, deployment, monitoring) are moving toward UI-based workflows. The catalog remains a manual, file-based outlier.\n* *Parity gap with Model Catalog.* The Model Catalog already provides an admin UI for managing catalog entries. MCP server catalog management should offer a comparable experience for the same admin personas.\n* *Operational risk in multi-team environments.* Without a structured UI, YAML edits are error-prone and lack validation, increasing the risk of misconfigured catalog entries in environments with multiple teams and namespaces.\n\nh2. Affected Customers\n\n* *Dell, Verizon, Costco* — Enterprise RHOAI customers with multi-team, multi-namespace environments managing MCP deployments at scale\n* *Enterprise platform teams* managing MCP deployments across multiple namespaces, teams, and environments who need operational visibility and lifecycle governance\n* *Security and compliance teams* who must audit which MCP servers are available in the catalog, trace their provenance, and verify policy compliance\n* *AI engineering teams* who need to understand which MCP servers are available within their namespace scope, what versions exist, and what lifecycle state they are in\n\nh2. Business Justification\n\n* *RHOAI 3.6 MCP Catalog GA readiness*: A YAML-only catalog management experience does not meet the usability bar for GA. Admin UI is required for end-to-end MCP management completeness.\n* *Consistency with MCP management direction*: The broader MCP lifecycle management story is converging on UI-driven workflows. The catalog is the remaining gap.\n* *Model Catalog parity*: The Model Catalog already serves the same admin personas with a UI experience. MCP Catalog admins expect the same.\n* *Strategic parent*: Contributes to RHAISTRAT-1791 (RHOAI 3.6 deliverables) for overall usability of end-to-end MCP management.\n\nh2. Acceptance Criteria\n\n* [ ] Administrators can add new MCP server entries to the catalog through the RHOAI UI without editing YAML\n* [ ] Administrators can edit existing MCP server catalog entries through the RHOAI UI\n* [ ] Administrators can remove MCP server entries from the catalog through the RHOAI UI\n* [ ] Catalog management operations are available to users with appropriate admin or operator roles consistent with existing RHOAI RBAC\n\nh2. Success Criteria\n\n* Platform operators and team leads can manage the MCP Catalog end-to-end through the UI without requiring YAML editing skills or developer assistance\n* MCP Catalog admin experience is consistent with the Model Catalog admin experience for comparable operations",
